"""Generated by make.py: (module, kind, question, model answer)."""
Q = [('1.1', 'Recall', 'Name the three quantities used to describe a moving crowd.', 'Density (people per m²), speed (m/s) and flow (people per second or minute, often per metre of width).'), ('1.1', 'Recall', 'What are the three phases of an event?', 'Ingress (arriving), circulation (moving around inside) and egress (leaving).'), ('1.1', 'Calculate', 'A gate passes 60 people a minute; 100 a minute arrive for 30 minutes. How many are waiting?', '(100 − 60) × 30 = 1,200 people.'), ('1.1', 'Explain', 'Why does the largest queue form after the peak of arrivals?', 'Because the queue keeps growing as long as arrivals exceed capacity, which continues for a while after the peak; it only shrinks once arrivals fall below capacity.'), ('1.1', 'Explain', "Why is 'panic' a misleading explanation for crowd crushes?", 'Most crush victims die from compressive asphyxia in crowds that are barely moving; research shows people mostly cooperate. The cause is too much density, created by failures of design, information and management.'), ('1.1', 'Apply', 'Give two things organisers can change to stop a queue growing at a gate.', 'Increase capacity (open more gates or lanes) or reduce or spread arrivals (staggered entry times, information, metering earlier on the route).'), ('1.2', 'Recall', 'Define density and give its unit.', 'The number of people per unit area, in people per square metre (per m²).'), ('1.2', 'Calculate', '300 people wait in a 100 m² area. What is the density and the space each?', '3 people per m²; 1 ÷ 3 ≈ 0.33 m² each.'), ('1.2', 'Recall', "What does Fruin's level of service A mean on a walkway?", 'More than about 3.25 m² per person: free choice of speed and path.'), ('1.2', 'Explain', 'Why do queuing areas use different density thresholds from walkways?', 'Standing people need less space than walking people, so they tolerate higher densities before discomfort; walking needs room for stride and passing.'), ('1.2', 'Calculate', 'How many people does a 1,600 m² usable area hold at 2 per m²?', '1,600 × 2 = 3,200 people.'), ('1.2', 'Apply', "A report says a square was 'only half full'. What would you ask?", 'Where the people were: was density uneven, with hot spots near stages, screens or exits much higher than the average?'), ('1.3', 'Recall', 'State the relation between flow, density and speed.', 'Specific flow J = density × speed, in people per metre of width per second; total flow = J × width.'), ('1.3', 'Calculate', 'A 3 m wide corridor has density 1.5 per m² and speed 0.8 m/s. What is the total flow?', 'J = 1.5 × 0.8 = 1.2 per m per s; total = 1.2 × 3 = 3.6 people per second.'), ('1.3', 'Explain', 'Why does flow peak and then fall as density rises?', "Flow is density times speed. At low density, adding people raises flow; but speed falls ever faster as people get in each other's way, so beyond a point the product falls."), ('1.3', 'Recall', "What is capacity in Weidmann's fundamental diagram, and at what density?", 'About 1.22 people per metre per second, at about 1.75 people per m².'), ('1.3', 'Apply', "A planner uses Weidmann's curve for elderly pilgrims. What is the risk?", 'Older or slower crowds have lower free speeds and capacities, so the plan may overestimate flow and underestimate queues and density.'), ('1.3', 'Explain', 'Why is a corridor operated near capacity fragile?', 'A small disturbance can push it onto the congested branch, where flow drops and density keeps rising, so it recovers only when demand falls.'), ('1.4', 'Recall', 'What is a bottleneck?', 'The narrowest point on a route, which limits the flow of the whole route.'), ('1.4', 'Calculate', 'A 1.5 m door has specific flow 1.3 per m per s. How long do 1,000 people take?', 'Capacity 1.95 per s; 1,000 ÷ 1.95 ≈ 513 s ≈ 8.5 minutes.'), ('1.4', 'Recall', 'What range of specific flows did bottleneck experiments find?', 'Roughly 1.3 to 1.9 people per metre per second (Seyfried et al. 2009).'), ('1.4', 'Explain', 'Why does widening the corridor before a narrow door not help?', 'The door still sets the capacity; extra width upstream only stores more of the queue.'), ('1.4', 'Calculate', 'Arrivals 4 per s, capacity 3 per s. How many are queuing after 15 minutes?', '(4 − 3) × 900 = 900 people.'), ('1.4', 'Apply', 'Name two places where a bottleneck is especially dangerous.', 'At the bottom of a slope or stair, where people above push forward; and where two flows meet head-on or at a T-junction.'), ('1.5', 'Recall', 'Name three self-organised crowd patterns.', 'Lane formation in counterflow, oscillations at narrow two-way doors, and stop-and-go waves at high density.'), ('1.5', 'Explain', 'How do lanes form without anyone planning them?', 'Walking behind someone going your way means fewer collisions; each person makes this small choice and the advantage spreads into lanes.'), ('1.5', 'Explain', 'Why are stop-and-go waves a warning sign?', 'They appear when density is near or above capacity, where small slowdowns grow; they can precede crowd turbulence, as at Jamarat in 2006.'), ('1.5', 'Apply', 'How would you remove oscillations at a narrow door used in both directions?', 'Make it one-way, for example separate entry and exit doors, or a central divider.'), ('1.5', 'Recall', 'In which direction do stop-and-go waves travel?', 'Backwards, against the walking direction.'), ('1.5', 'Apply', 'A control room sees lanes turn into stop-and-go motion. What should happen?', 'Treat it as a sign of density near capacity: reduce or stop inflow, open relief routes and inform the crowd before turbulence develops.'), ('1.6', 'Recall', 'What is affiliation in exit choice?', 'The tendency to move towards familiar places and people, so people often leave by the route they entered.'), ('1.6', 'Explain', "Why is 'mass panic' a poor explanation of crowd disasters?", 'Evidence shows people mostly cooperate; crush deaths come from physical density and pressure caused by design, information and management failures.'), ('1.6', 'Calculate', '1,200 people, two exits of 3 per s each, 80% use one. Roughly when is the busy exit empty?', '960 ÷ 3 = 320 s plus about 20 s walking ≈ 5.7 minutes.'), ('1.6', 'Recall', 'Name two ways groups change crowd movement.', 'Groups walk at the pace of their slowest member, form shapes such as lines or Vs, and gather before evacuating, adding pre-movement time.'), ('1.6', 'Apply', 'How could you improve information for a multilingual crowd?', 'Use pictograms and colour codes, signs in the main languages, group leaders who brief their groups, and visible stewards.'), ('1.6', 'Explain', 'Why do people at the back push forward even when the front is crushed?', 'They cannot see or hear what is happening at the front, so they keep moving towards their goal.'), ('1.7', 'Recall', 'At what density do people lose the ability to move independently?', 'Above about 5 to 6 people per m².'), ('1.7', 'Recall', 'Define crowd pressure.', 'Local density multiplied by the variance of local velocity, P = ρ × Var(V) (Helbing et al. 2007).'), ('1.7', 'Explain', 'Describe the chain of events leading to a crowd crush.', 'Demand exceeds capacity, density rises, people lose control and forces add up through bodies, turbulence pushes people over, falls spread into a crowd collapse, and people die of compressive asphyxia.'), ('1.7', 'Recall', 'What happened at the Love Parade in 2010?', 'In Duisburg, 21 people died at a ramp that was the only way in and out of the festival area, where incoming and outgoing flows met.'), ('1.7', 'Apply', 'Name three warning signs control-room staff can watch for.', 'Stop-and-go waves, swaying surges or turbulence, people climbing out over barriers, and distress or emergency calls.'), ('1.7', 'Explain', 'Why is the 0.02 per s² pressure value not a design standard?', "It comes from one site's video data and measurement method; values depend on how density and velocity are measured, so it is a research indicator, not a universal limit."), ('1.8', 'Recall', 'What does DIM-ICE stand for?', 'Design, Information, Management crossed with Ingress, Circulation, Egress.'), ('1.8', 'Recall', 'What does RAMP stand for?', 'Routes, Areas, Movement, Profile.'), ('1.8', 'Calculate', '4,000 people, stepped route, 8 minutes. Exit width?', '4,000 ÷ (66 × 8) ≈ 7.6 m.'), ('1.8', 'Explain', 'Why does the same space need different plans for different crowds?', 'Profiles differ: age, mobility, culture, familiarity, purpose and group size change speeds, routes and behaviour.'), ('1.8', 'Apply', 'Name four measures that keep density down on the day.', 'Separating in and out flows, one-way systems, holding areas and metering, staggered times, clear multilingual information, and empowered stewards.'), ('1.8', 'Explain', 'Why is egress often the hardest phase?', 'Everyone wants to leave at once at the end, so the peak flow is concentrated in a short time, often with tired people and darkness.'), ('2.1', 'Recall', 'What is a trajectory in crowd measurement?', 'The sequence of positions (x, y) of one person over time, usually extracted from video.'), ('2.1', 'Explain', 'Why is Voronoi density smoother than classical density?', 'Because each person contributes the fraction of their cell inside the area, density changes gradually as people move, instead of jumping by 1 ÷ A when someone crosses the boundary.'), ('2.1', 'Calculate', '120 people cross a line in 60 s in a 4 m wide corridor. Specific flow?', '120 ÷ 60 = 2 per s; ÷ 4 m = 0.5 per m per s.'), ('2.1', 'Explain', 'How can J = ρ × v help you check data?', 'In steady flow, the measured flow should equal measured density times speed; a big mismatch points to errors in the measurement area, time window or tracking.'), ('2.1', 'Recall', 'Name two open resources for crowd trajectories and analysis.', 'The Jülich pedestrian dynamics data archive and the PedPy Python library (PeTrack for extracting trajectories from video).'), ('2.1', 'Apply', 'At a festival without overhead cameras, how could you estimate density?', 'Combine gate counts with area measurements, CCTV with computer-vision counting, and phone-based data, and calibrate each against manual counts.'), ('2.2', 'Recall', 'Write the conservation rule for people in a cell.', 'Change in people = inflow − outflow; in density form ρᵢ(t+Δt) = ρᵢ(t) + (Δt/Δx)(f_in − f_out).'), ('2.2', 'Explain', 'What are demand and supply in the cell transmission model?', 'Demand is the flow a cell can send (J(ρ) below critical density, capacity above); supply is what it can receive (capacity below critical, J(ρ) above). The flow between cells is the smaller.'), ('2.2', 'Calculate', 'Corridor capacity 1.22 per m per s; a bottleneck half as wide. Its capacity per metre of corridor width?', 'About 0.61 per s.'), ('2.2', 'Explain', 'Why does the tail of a jam move backwards?', 'People keep arriving at the back faster than the bottleneck releases them, so the congested region grows upstream.'), ('2.2', 'Recall', 'What did Hughes (2002) add to macroscopic crowd models?', 'A 2-D continuum model where people move down the gradient of travel time to their goal, accounting for density ahead.'), ('2.2', 'Apply', 'When would you choose a macroscopic model over a microscopic one?', 'For large networks, many scenarios or real-time forecasts, where speed matters more than individual detail.'), ('2.3', 'Recall', 'What size are cells in a typical crowd CA, and why?', 'About 0.4 m × 0.4 m, roughly the space one person occupies in a dense crowd (6.25 per m² full).'), ('2.3', 'Explain', 'What do the static and dynamic floor fields represent?', "The static field is distance to the exit (a fixed slope towards it); the dynamic field is a fading trail of others' movements that people are drawn to follow."), ('2.3', 'Calculate', 'Cells of 0.4 m, steps of 0.3 s. What is the free speed?', '0.4 ÷ 0.3 ≈ 1.33 m/s.'), ('2.3', 'Explain', 'Why does a plain CA overestimate exit flow?', 'With sequential update and removal at exit cells, one person can leave each exit cell every step, giving several people per metre per second, far above the 1.3–1.9 measured.'), ('2.3', 'Apply', "How could you make a CA's exit flow realistic?", 'Calibrate: add friction with parallel update, or an exit probability, and tune it until the exit flow matches experiments (about 1.3–1.9 per m per s).'), ('2.3', 'Recall', 'What does kD > 0 reproduce?', 'Following and herding: people tend to step where others have just walked, which forms lanes and trails.'), ('2.4', 'Recall', 'Name the three kinds of term in the social force model.', 'A driving force towards the desired velocity, interaction forces with other people, and forces from walls and obstacles.'), ('2.4', 'Explain', 'What does the relaxation time τ do?', "It sets how quickly a person's velocity adapts to the desired velocity: the driving force is m(v₀e − v)/τ, so a small τ means a quick reaction."), ('2.4', 'Explain', 'How does the model produce faster-is-slower?', 'Higher desired speeds push people into the door harder; contact and friction forces form arches that block the exit, so the outflow becomes intermittent and slower.'), ('2.4', 'Recall', 'Give typical values for A, B and τ.', 'A ≈ 2,000 N, B ≈ 0.08 m, τ ≈ 0.5 s (Helbing, Farkas and Vicsek 2000).'), ('2.4', 'Explain', 'Why does the social force model need small time steps?', 'Contact forces are very stiff (k ≈ 10⁵), so large steps let people overlap or pass through each other; steps of about 0.005 s keep it stable.'), ('2.4', 'Apply', 'Give two weaknesses to mention when reporting social force results.', 'Many parameters that need calibration, and numerical sensitivity (time step, overlaps, oscillations); also speeds may not fall realistically at high density without extra terms.'), ('2.5', 'Recall', 'List the seven model families in order of detail.', 'Hydraulic, macroscopic, cellular automata, force-based, velocity-based, optimal steps, cognitive heuristics.'), ('2.5', 'Explain', 'How does the collision-free speed model choose a speed?', 'As the largest speed that avoids a collision within time T: v = min(v₀, max(0, (s − ℓ)/T)), with s the gap and ℓ the body length.'), ('2.5', 'Recall', 'What are the two heuristics of Moussaïd, Helbing and Theraulaz?', 'Choose the direction that brings you closest to your goal before a collision, and walk at a speed that keeps a time gap to the first obstacle.'), ('2.5', 'Apply', 'Which family for a city-wide festival network with many scenarios?', 'A macroscopic or network model (or a calibrated CA) for speed, possibly with microscopic models for hot spots.'), ('2.5', 'Explain', 'Why compare models on the same scenario?', 'Different models can give different results for the same layout; comparing them shows how much conclusions depend on model choice, a form of model uncertainty.'), ('2.5', 'Recall', 'Which open simulator implements the optimal steps model?', 'Vadere (JuPedSim implements velocity-based and force-based models).'), ('2.6', 'Recall', 'What is a quickest-path route choice rule?', 'Choose the route with the smallest expected time: walking time plus expected waiting time.'), ('2.6', 'Calculate', 'Exit A: 30 m, exit B: 90 m, 4 per s each, 1.2 m/s, 2,000 people, all to A. Time?', '2,000 ÷ 4 = 500 s plus 25 s walking ≈ 525 s ≈ 8.8 min.'), ('2.6', 'Explain', 'Why is the best split the one where both exits finish together?', 'If one exit finished earlier, moving someone to it from the other would shorten the total; at the optimum no move helps, so finishing times are equal.'), ('2.6', 'Recall', 'Define user equilibrium and system optimum.', 'User equilibrium: no one can reduce their own time by switching. System optimum: the routing that minimises a total measure for all.'), ('2.6', 'Explain', "What does Braess's paradox teach crowd planners?", "Adding a new route can increase everyone's travel time if people choose selfishly; more connections are not always better."), ('2.6', 'Apply', 'How would you guide people towards the better exit?', 'Early, visible, trusted guidance: dynamic signs showing waiting times or open exits, stewards at decision points, and clear announcements.'), ('2.7', 'Recall', 'List the four parts of RSET.', 'Detection, warning, pre-movement and movement time.'), ('2.7', 'Calculate', 'Detection 60 s, warning 30 s, pre-movement 120 s, movement 192 s. RSET?', '60 + 30 + 120 + 192 = 402 s ≈ 6.7 min.'), ('2.7', 'Explain', 'Why is pre-movement time often the largest uncertainty?', 'It depends on human recognition and response (alertness, familiarity, staff, the alarm message, groups), which vary widely and give long-tailed distributions.'), ('2.7', 'Calculate', 'Movement: 60 m at 1.2 m/s, or 1,500 people through 6 m at 1.3 per m per s. Which applies?', 'Walking 50 s, flow about 192 s, so movement ≈ 192 s (the longer).'), ('2.7', 'Explain', 'Why treat RSET as a distribution?', 'Inputs such as pre-movement, speeds and flows are uncertain; sampling them shows the range of outcomes, and safety depends on the slow tail.'), ('2.7', 'Apply', 'How could you shorten RSET without widening exits?', 'Faster detection, clearer voice warnings with instructions, trained staff who start the evacuation, which all reduce pre-movement time.'), ('2.8', 'Recall', 'Distinguish verification, calibration and validation.', 'Verification: code implements the equations. Calibration: fit parameters to data. Validation: test the calibrated model on independent data.'), ('2.8', 'Explain', 'Why calibrate on several observables at once?', 'A model can match one quantity (such as the fundamental diagram) while getting others (bottleneck flow, travel times) wrong; several targets constrain the parameters better.'), ('2.8', 'Calculate', 'Model speeds 1.0, 0.8, 0.5; measured 1.1, 0.7, 0.5. RMSE?', 'Errors −0.1, 0.1, 0; RMSE = √((0.01 + 0.01 + 0)/3) ≈ 0.082 m/s.'), ('2.8', 'Explain', 'What is overfitting and how do you detect it?', 'Fitting noise in the calibration data so well that predictions on new data are poor; detect it by validating on held-out data.'), ('2.8', 'Recall', 'Name two sources of standard tests for evacuation simulators.', 'The German RiMEA guideline and the NIST verification and validation tests (TN 1822).'), ('2.8', 'Apply', 'What would you report about uncertainty in a simulation study?', 'Parameter ranges and sensitivity, spread across repeated stochastic runs, results from a second model if possible, and what the model cannot represent.'), ('3.1', 'Recall', 'Give the four parts of a PICO question.', 'Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome.'), ('3.1', 'Explain', 'Why is risk exposure better than peak density as an outcome?', 'It combines how long and how many people spent time above a threshold, distinguishing a brief spike from a long dangerous period.'), ('3.1', 'Calculate', '1,000 m² at 5 per m² for 12 minutes, all above a 4 per m² threshold. Exposure?', '5 × 1,000 × 12 = 60,000 person-minutes.'), ('3.1', 'Explain', 'Why use a factorial design instead of changing one factor at a time?', 'It captures interactions, for example a layout that helps only when schedules slip.'), ('3.1', 'Apply', 'What ethics measures would you plan for CCTV data at a pilgrimage?', 'Data minimisation, anonymisation (for example counting without identifying), secure storage, approval from authorities and ethics boards, and clear purposes.'), ('3.1', 'Explain', 'Why are mass gatherings especially hard to study?', 'Experiments are impossible at full scale, data are scarce and sensitive, demand is synchronised by rituals or schedules, and crowds are diverse.'), ('3.2', 'Recall', 'What happened at Hillsborough in 1989?', 'A gate was opened to relieve congestion outside the stadium and supporters flowed into central pens that were already full; 97 people died.'), ('3.2', 'Explain', 'Why are crowd disasters called systemic failures?', 'Because many weaknesses in design, information and management interact and align; there is rarely a single cause or culprit.'), ('3.2', 'Recall', 'What was distinctive about the Love Parade site?', 'A single ramp, reached through tunnels, was the only entrance and exit of the festival area, so inflow and outflow met there.'), ('3.2', 'Explain', 'Why do death counts for Mina 2015 differ so much?', 'The official Saudi count (769) was never updated, while independent tallies from national governments and media exceeded 2,000; data access and transparency were limited.'), ('3.2', 'Apply', 'How would you score Itaewon in DIM-ICE?', 'High risk in almost every cell: no designed routes or one-way systems, no information to the crowd, and no event management for ingress, circulation or egress.'), ('3.2', 'Explain', 'What is hindsight bias and why does it matter?', 'Judging past decisions with knowledge decision makers did not have; it can lead to blaming individuals instead of fixing the information and systems they relied on.'), ('3.3', 'Recall', 'Why is Hajj demand called synchronised?', 'Because the ritual sequence fixes the days and places of the rites, so very large numbers of pilgrims need the same places at nearly the same times.'), ('3.3', 'Recall', 'How was the Jamarat Bridge redesigned after 2006?', 'As a multi-storey structure with several levels, wide ramps and one-way flows so that streams to and from the pillars do not meet.'), ('3.3', 'Calculate', '450,000 pilgrims, 6 equal slots of one hour, route capacity 60,000 per hour. Is it enough?', 'No: 75,000 per slot exceeds 60,000; use more slots or routes.'), ('3.3', 'Explain', 'Why does non-compliance hurt scheduling so much?', 'Groups that leave early tend to choose the same popular times, concentrating extra demand in a few slots, which can push them far over capacity.'), ('3.3', 'Apply', 'Name two research questions for Hajj crowd management.', 'How robust schedules are to non-compliance and delays; how real-time monitoring should adjust release times; how heat and age affect speeds and risk; how to use camera and phone data with privacy.'), ('3.3', 'Explain', 'Why do pilgrim groups make scheduling possible?', 'Because groups have leaders and fixed organisers, authorities can assign and communicate time slots and routes to each group, unlike an anonymous crowd.'), ('3.4', 'Recall', "State Jacobs' method.", "Divide the area into zones, multiply each zone's area by its estimated density, and add the zones."), ('3.4', 'Calculate', '10,000 m² at 2 per m² and 20,000 m² at 0.5 per m². Crowd size?', '20,000 + 10,000 = 30,000 people.'), ('3.4', 'Explain', 'How does a density-map network count a crowd?', 'It predicts for each pixel the expected number of heads; summing the map gives the count, avoiding the need to detect every person.'), ('3.4', 'Explain', 'Why report an interval instead of one number?', 'Densities, areas and model outputs are uncertain; an interval shows how far the true size could be from the estimate.'), ('3.4', 'Apply', 'What biases affect phone-based crowd estimates?', 'Not everyone carries a phone or has it switched on; operators, settings and roaming differ; children and older people may be under-represented.'), ('3.4', 'Apply', 'List three privacy safeguards for CCTV-based counting.', 'Count without identifying, aggregate to density per cell, keep data briefly and securely, and obtain approvals.'), ('3.5', 'Recall', 'Give the formula for crowd pressure in a cell.', 'P = local density × variance of local velocity over a short time window.'), ('3.5', 'Explain', 'Why tie warning levels to predefined actions?', 'Because a warning without a planned response and an authorised decision maker leads to delay; predefined actions can start immediately.'), ('3.5', 'Explain', 'What is lead time and why does it matter?', 'The time between a warning and danger; only warnings with enough lead time allow inflow to be stopped and crowds relieved.'), ('3.5', 'Calculate', 'A cell has density 6 per m² and speed variance 0.004 m²/s². Pressure?', '6 × 0.004 = 0.024 per s², above a 0.02 action level.'), ('3.5', 'Apply', 'Name two pitfalls of camera-based monitoring.', 'Incomplete coverage, poor estimates in very dense crowds or bad light, false alarms eroding trust, operator overload, and warnings without response.'), ('3.5', 'Explain', 'How can forecasts help a control room?', 'Models fed by live counts can predict density 10–30 minutes ahead, so staff can act before thresholds are reached.'), ('3.6', 'Recall', 'List the main steps of a crowd simulation study.', 'Geometry, demand, model choice and calibration, runs with several seeds, sensitivity analysis, outputs with spread, and documentation.'), ('3.6', 'Explain', 'Why check geometry on site?', 'Temporary stalls, barriers, vehicles and closures change effective widths and routes, so plans alone can overestimate capacity.'), ('3.6', 'Recall', 'What flows did the JuPedSim gate lab give?', 'About 1.05 to 1.18 people per metre per second, consistent with laboratory experiments.'), ('3.6', 'Explain', 'What is the purpose of a sensitivity analysis?', 'To see how much outputs change with uncertain inputs, identify which inputs matter most, and report results with honest ranges.'), ('3.6', 'Apply', 'Why couple a macroscopic model with microscopic ones for the Hajj?', 'The whole site and timetable need a fast network model, but ramps, junctions and gates need individual detail; coupling gives both.'), ('3.6', 'Apply', 'What would you share to make a simulation study reproducible?', 'Scripts, geometry and parameter files, input data (or how to get it), simulator versions and random seeds.'), ('3.7', 'Recall', 'Name four types of crowd intervention.', 'Metering, one-way systems, holding areas, scheduling, information and stewarding, design changes.'), ('3.7', 'Explain', 'Why do interventions move risk rather than delete it?', 'Because demand still has to go somewhere: metering creates queues upstream, holding areas store people, one-way systems lengthen routes; the new places must be safe.'), ('3.7', 'Calculate', 'Arrivals peak at 320 per minute, venue takes 180, metering at 200. Roughly how fast does the outside queue grow at the peak?', 'About 320 − 200 = 120 people per minute.'), ('3.7', 'Explain', 'What makes an evaluation fair?', 'Same demand, same model and outputs, several seeds and sensitivity cases, comparing intervention and baseline, and reporting exposure in all places.'), ('3.7', 'Apply', 'Name two unintended effects to test for when adding barriers.', 'People taking short-cuts around them, new bottlenecks at barrier ends, longer routes that cause counterflows, and holding areas filling too fast.'), ('3.7', 'Apply', 'When is metering a good idea?', 'When the space upstream is open, wide and managed, so waiting there is safer than in the enclosed space downstream.'), ('3.8', 'Recall', 'Name three open research fronts in crowd dynamics.', 'Pushing and forces at extreme density; social psychology and culture in models; AI for monitoring; digital twins; heat and health at mass gatherings.'), ('3.8', 'Explain', 'Why is combining physics and social psychology important?', 'Crowd movement depends on norms, identity and groups as well as space and forces; models that ignore them can mispredict behaviour, especially in emergencies.'), ('3.8', 'Explain', 'What is false precision and how do you avoid it?', 'Reporting more digits than the uncertainty supports; report rounded values with ranges from seeds and sensitivity.'), ('3.8', 'Apply', 'What should a results section say about model limits?', 'What the model cannot represent (for example pushing, groups or information), how that could bias the results, and which assumptions would change the conclusions.'), ('3.8', 'Explain', 'Why can a simulation not prove a site is safe?', 'Results depend on assumptions about demand, behaviour and parameters; a simulation can only show conditional performance under stated assumptions and margins.'), ('3.8', 'Apply', 'How would you communicate a finding to an event authority?', 'A clear message, ranges rather than single numbers, the key assumptions, what would change the conclusion, and practical implications.')]
